scope: |
  Sergiy asked to tighten claim discipline after TWO collisions on 2026-07-27:
  (A) post-f4-board-reconcile vs f4-arc-closure — same work, different slugs, ~2 min apart;
  (B) v2-f5b-typed-locals did batch C while scljet-ipk-rowid held it on origin/main.
  Diagnosis: the existing check greps for the EXACT slug filename, so same-work-different-slug is
  invisible; and because each claim writes a DISJOINT file, the loser of the push race rebases
  cleanly and never sees the winner's claim. Nothing checks that commits stay inside a claim.
  Deliverables (Sergiy chose the full set):
    1. .work/active/LEDGER.tsv — one shared append line per claim, so a concurrent claim CONFLICTS
       at rebase instead of silently auto-merging (the only non-bypassable layer).
    2. claims declare items/bugs/paths; a pre-push hook refuses a claim overlapping a live one.
    3. pre-commit scope guard: in feature/<slug>, refuse staged paths outside the claim's paths.
    4. invert the protocol to claim-BEFORE-plan (AGENTS.md; skill lives in the .agents/plugins
       submodule — mirror there or note it).
  Every hook must be PROVEN to fail loudly (AGENTS.md: build the gate before trusting it).
next: WIDENED paths (tests/coord/, .github/workflows/ci.yml) — the new scope guard refused
  my own first commit for exactly these, which is the dogfood proof it works. Spec landed; hooks,
  ledger, coord-claim and both test scripts are written and green (10/10, all refuse-cases proven
  red when the hooks are neutered). Remaining: land + CI.
